    "geoSummary": "llms.txt operating policy is useful only when it becomes an operating decision, not a trend label. This piece maps the source evidence, the implementation risk and the reviewer who must own quality. The practical next step is to test whether the team can turn AI readability into website governance with clear metrics and rollback paths.",
    "body": "llms.txt operating policy looks like a market story, but the useful column starts with a narrower question: what would make a serious team change behavior this month? If the answer is vague, the topic belongs on the watchlist. If it is concrete, it deserves an experiment.\n\nLatest context: Google AI published \"Check out real-life AI prototypes from the Futures Lab.\" on May 29, 2026. We also check Google AI's \"Catch up on 12 major I/O 2026 moments\" so the piece is not built from a single headline.\n\n## The decision hiding inside the trend\n\nTeams do not need more AI vocabulary. They need to know whether llms.txt operating policy changes a customer promise, a workflow owner, a cost line or a review obligation.\n\n## What the sources support\n\n- Google AI: Check out real-life AI prototypes from the Futures Lab. (May 29, 2026) — <img…\n- Google AI: Catch up on 12 major I/O 2026 moments (May 28, 2026) — <img…\n- Google AI: Catch up on the Dialogues stage at Google I/O 2026. (May 22, 2026) — <img…\n- Google Search Central: AI features and your website\n\n## Operator frame\n\n| Lens | Useful question | Editorial output |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Market | What actually changed around llms.txt operating policy? | Separate source facts from interpretation. |\n| Reader | What decision does the operator need to make? | Give a direct answer before analysis. |\n| Risk | What could be wrong or early? | Mark uncertainty and avoid fake precision. |\n| Action | What is the smallest next step? | Translate the signal into how to turn AI readability into website governance. |\n\n## The tradeoff\n\nMoving early can create advantage when the evidence is official, repeatable and close to a live workflow. Moving early becomes theater when the team cannot say who reviews the output or what failure looks like.\n\n## ALTOS LAB point of view\n\nOur stance is to be opinionated but not louder than the evidence. A good column on llms.txt operating policy should leave the reader with a cleaner decision, not just a stronger feeling that AI is important.",
